-- LEVEL 13: CS Fundamentals - Advanced System Concepts (mcq)
(13, 'CAP Theorem In Distributed Systems', 'According to the CAP Theorem, what can a distributed system guarantee in the presence of a network partition? (A) Both Consistency and Availability (B) Either Consistency OR Availability (C) Neither (D) Infinite scalability', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Consistent Hashing Ring Node Addition', 'In consistent hashing, what fraction of keys need to be remapped when a new node is added to an n-node cluster? (A) 100% (B) 50% (C) Approximately 1/n (D) None', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Two-Phase Commit (2PC) Vulnerability', 'What is the main drawback of the Two-Phase Commit protocol? (A) High network throughput (B) Blocking coordinator single point of failure (C) Inability to write SQL (D) Lack of atomicity', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Raft Consensus Leader Election', 'In the Raft consensus algorithm, how does a candidate node become leader? (A) Longest running uptime (B) Receiving votes from a majority of cluster nodes (C) Lowest IP address (D) Manual operator trigger', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'LSM Tree (Log-Structured Merge-Tree) Mechanics', 'Why do write-heavy databases (Cassandra/RocksDB) use LSM Trees over B-Trees? (A) Sequential disk writes to append-only memtable/SSTables (B) Faster random point reads (C) No memory usage (D) Automatic schema validation', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'TCP Head-of-Line (HoL) Blocking in HTTP/2', 'What causes Head-of-Line blocking at the transport layer in HTTP/2 multiplexing? (A) Browser rendering lag (B) A single dropped TCP packet halts all multiplexed streams until retransmitted (C) SSL handshake latency (D) Header compression failure', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Bloom Filter False Positives vs False Negatives', 'Which characteristic is true for a standard Bloom Filter? (A) False positives are possible, but false negatives are impossible (B) False negatives are possible, but false positives are impossible (C) Both are impossible (D) Exact counts are stored', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Zero-Copy IO and the sendfile System Call', 'How does the sendfile syscall achieve zero-copy data transfer? (A) Copies data through user-space RAM (B) Transfers data directly from OS page cache to network socket buffer (C) Uses GPU compute (D) Disables encryption', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Optimistic vs Pessimistic Concurrency Control', 'When is Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC) preferred over Pessimistic Locking? (A) High contention with frequent conflicting writes (B) Low write contention with predominantly read workloads (C) Single-threaded systems (D) Unindexed tables', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Database Phantom Reads Isolation Level', 'Which standard SQL transaction isolation level is required to completely prevent Phantom Reads? (A) Read Uncommitted (B) Read Committed (C) Repeatable Read (D) Serializable', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Epoll vs Select / Poll in Linux Network Programming', 'Why is epoll O(1) in handling millions of concurrent socket connections compared to select O(N)? (A) Epoll polls every file descriptor (B) Epoll uses kernel event callbacks returning only ready descriptors (C) Epoll runs on UDP only (D) Select uses multi-threading', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'CRDTs (Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types)', 'What mathematical property ensures CRDT replicas converge to the same state without central coordination? (A) Commutative, Associative, and Idempotent state merges (B) Round-robin synchronization (C) Global clock synchronization (D) Master-slave election', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'QUIC Protocol & UDP Migration in HTTP/3', 'What is the primary motivation for HTTP/3 transitioning from TCP to QUIC over UDP? (A) Eliminate connection migration (B) Independent stream recovery without transport HoL blocking and faster 0-RTT handshakes (C) Replace TLS encryption (D) Reduce server CPU', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Vector Clocks for Causality Tracking', 'What can Vector Clocks detect in distributed systems that simple timestamps cannot? (A) Concurrent conflicting updates (B) Network throughput (C) CPU temperature (D) Disk latency', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Cache Invalidation: Write-Through vs Write-Back', 'What is the risk of Write-Back (Write-Behind) caching strategy? (A) Slow write latency (B) Potential data loss if cache node crashes before flushing dirty writes to DB (C) High database write load (D) No cache hits', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Circuit Breaker State Transitions', 'In what state does a circuit breaker allow a limited number of probe requests to check if downstream health recovered? (A) Closed (B) Open (C) Half-Open (D) Tripped', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'False Sharing in Multi-Core CPU Caches', 'What causes CPU cache false sharing in concurrent programming? (A) Incompatible RAM speeds (B) Independent variables accessed by different threads residing on the same 64-byte cache line (C) Clock jitter (D) Cache misses on L3', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Garbage Collection Stop-The-World Pauses', 'What technique do modern low-latency GCs (Go runtime / Java ZGC) use to avoid long STW pauses? (A) Concurrent marking and incremental evacuation alongside application threads (B) Disabling pointer checks (C) Deallocating on thread exit (D) Fixed heap sizes', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Actor Model Concurrency', 'How do Actor Model entities (Erlang / Akka) communicate with each other? (A) Shared memory variables (B) Asynchronous message passing to immutable mailboxes (C) Global mutex locks (D) Database polling', 'Medium', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),
(13, 'Database Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) Guarantee', 'Why must Write-Ahead Log entries be flushed to disk before updating in-memory database pages? (A) To compress table data (B) To guarantee durability and enable crash recovery via ARIES replay (C) To update indexes (D) To release read locks', 'Hard', 'mcq', NOW(), NOW()),

